1
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:23,180
Throughout your laboratory experiences, you will frequently need to assess the purity

2
00:00:23,180 --> 00:00:26,080
or identity of a crystalline solid.

3
00:00:26,080 --> 00:00:30,700
Both of these goals can be accomplished by determining a compound's melting point.

4
00:00:30,700 --> 00:00:36,020
When a compound is in a crystalline solid state, the intermolecular forces that are holding

5
00:00:36,020 --> 00:00:40,980
the molecules together greatly outweigh the kinetic energy that is trying to pull them

6
00:00:40,980 --> 00:00:41,980
apart.

7
00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:47,300
Therefore, the molecules appear to vibrate in a fixed location.

8
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:52,420
If energy is introduced into the system in the form of heat, the kinetic energy of the

9
00:00:52,420 --> 00:00:57,380
molecules and thus the temperature of the system is increased.

10
00:00:57,380 --> 00:01:02,820
At a certain point, the molecular kinetic energy is high enough to overcome the attractive

11
00:01:02,820 --> 00:01:06,300
forces that are holding the molecules together.

12
00:01:06,300 --> 00:01:12,460
As a result, the crystal lattice breaks down and the molecules enter a liquid state.

13
00:01:12,460 --> 00:01:17,740
The temperature at which the solid and liquid phases of a compound are in equilibrium at

14
00:01:17,740 --> 00:01:22,140
a certain pressure is called the melting point.

15
00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:28,700
In reality, a solid melts over a temperature range instead of at a specific temperature.

16
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:34,960
Because this range is a physical characteristic, it is reproducible for a pure compound.

17
00:01:34,960 --> 00:01:43,680
For example, N-phenylacetamin melts between 114.2 and 114.9 degrees Celsius.

18
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:49,160
Therefore, the term melting point really implies a melting range.

19
00:01:49,160 --> 00:01:53,840
Although a variety of melting point apparatuses are commercially available, this video will

20
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:58,960
demonstrate the determination of melting point by using the meltump.

21
00:01:58,960 --> 00:02:04,760
The meltump apparatus consists of an on-off switch, a sample holder, a light source that

22
00:02:04,760 --> 00:02:10,480
illuminates the sample, an eyepiece that magnifies the sample, a digital thermometer, which indicates

23
00:02:10,480 --> 00:02:13,960
the temperature of the system, and a voltage control.

24
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:19,440
The voltage control dictates the rate of heating, with a higher setting corresponding to a faster

25
00:02:19,440 --> 00:02:20,480
rate.

26
00:02:20,480 --> 00:02:25,020
The voltage control does not control the temperature of the system.

27
00:02:25,020 --> 00:02:30,040
Use caution when touching the meltump and always assume that the apparatus is hot, especially

28
00:02:30,040 --> 00:02:33,800
the part that is labeled caution, unit hot.

29
00:02:33,840 --> 00:02:38,840
The first step in determining a melting point is the preparation of the crystalline solid.

30
00:02:38,840 --> 00:02:44,800
To prepare the sample, you will need a spatula, a watch glass, a mortar and pestle, capillary

31
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:49,400
tubes that are sealed at one end, and a long piece of glass tubing, such as a long stem

32
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:51,080
funnel.

33
00:02:51,080 --> 00:02:55,800
Before proceeding, ensure that the crystalline solid is clean and dry.

34
00:02:55,800 --> 00:03:00,000
If the solid is wet, you can try to pat it dry with filter paper.

35
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:05,480
Do not place the sample in the oven, where it may melt or decompose.

36
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:10,640
If your sample consists of large crystals, you must first crush them into smaller crystals

37
00:03:10,640 --> 00:03:13,120
with a mortar and pestle.

38
00:03:13,120 --> 00:03:17,320
Large crystals cannot pack together well, which creates air pockets.

39
00:03:17,320 --> 00:03:22,680
If air pockets are present in your sample, then a time lag for heat transfer will exist,

40
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:25,600
causing the crystals to heat unevenly.

41
00:03:25,600 --> 00:03:30,640
If you do not have a mortar and pestle, you can use the flat end of a spatula to grind

42
00:03:30,640 --> 00:03:33,120
the sample instead.

43
00:03:33,120 --> 00:03:36,800
After crushing the crystals, place them on a watch glass.

44
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:42,000
Turn the capillary tube upside down and thrust the open end over the crystals.

45
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:46,240
A small amount of crystals should be trapped inside the capillary.

46
00:03:46,240 --> 00:03:51,360
Invert the tube to its correct orientation and pack the crystals down to the bottom

47
00:03:51,360 --> 00:03:56,080
by gently stroking the tube or by tapping it on your benchtop.

48
00:03:56,080 --> 00:04:00,920
If the crystals fail to fall to the bottom of the capillary, then drop the capillary

49
00:04:00,920 --> 00:04:03,320
through a length of glass tubing.

50
00:04:03,320 --> 00:04:07,160
A long stem funnel works well for this application.

51
00:04:07,160 --> 00:04:12,160
You may have to repeat this action several times to ensure that the crystals are effectively

52
00:04:12,160 --> 00:04:14,320
packed down.

53
00:04:14,320 --> 00:04:19,400
Loose material contains air pockets, which cause the sample to heat unevenly.

54
00:04:19,400 --> 00:04:25,000
Ideally, the smallest manageable amount of material should be used, which correlates to

55
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:29,440
a depth of approximately 1 millimeter of packed material.

56
00:04:29,440 --> 00:04:36,240
If the depth exceeds 2 millimeters, inaccuracies may result due to uneven heating.

57
00:04:36,240 --> 00:04:39,720
Prepare four capillary tubes of your sample.

58
00:04:39,720 --> 00:04:44,200
One filled capillary tube will be used to determine a crude melting point, and the

59
00:04:44,200 --> 00:04:49,200
other three will be used to determine your actual melting point in triplicate.

60
00:04:49,200 --> 00:04:53,880
If you do not know the expected melting point of your compound, you must first determine a

61
00:04:53,880 --> 00:04:55,960
crude melting point.

62
00:04:55,960 --> 00:05:00,720
Finding the approximate melting point of the sample will save a lot of time when performing

63
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:02,440
the actual measurement.

64
00:05:02,440 --> 00:05:07,080
To determine the crude melting point, set the voltage on the melt temp to zero and

65
00:05:07,080 --> 00:05:11,200
wait until the apparatus has cooled to room temperature.

66
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:17,320
Insert a sample, increase the temperature at a rate of 10 to 20 degrees Celsius per minute,

67
00:05:17,320 --> 00:05:21,600
and record a rough temperature when the sample melts.

68
00:05:21,600 --> 00:05:26,040
To determine the melting point of a crystalline solid, set the voltage on the melt temp to

69
00:05:26,040 --> 00:05:31,720
zero, turn on the power and the digital thermometer, and then insert a filled capillary tube into

70
00:05:31,720 --> 00:05:33,360
the sample holder.

71
00:05:33,360 --> 00:05:39,360
If the temperature is lower than 20 degrees Celsius below the expected melting point,

72
00:05:39,360 --> 00:05:43,960
then dial the voltage to a setting that enables the temperature to increase at a rate

73
00:05:43,960 --> 00:05:47,400
of 10 to 20 degrees Celsius per minute.

74
00:05:47,400 --> 00:05:52,440
After the temperature is within 20 degrees Celsius of the expected melting point, dial

75
00:05:52,440 --> 00:05:57,720
down the voltage so that the temperature increases at a rate of 1 to 2 degrees Celsius

76
00:05:57,720 --> 00:05:59,600
per minute.

77
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:03,880
Watch the sample through the eyepiece and record the temperature when the first crystal

78
00:06:03,880 --> 00:06:08,840
starts to melt, which means when the first droplet of liquid appears.

79
00:06:08,840 --> 00:06:13,840
Finally, record the temperature when the last crystal disappears.

80
00:06:13,840 --> 00:06:18,280
Bring down the voltage, remove the melted sample, and throw it into the proper waste

81
00:06:18,280 --> 00:06:20,040
receptacle.

82
00:06:20,040 --> 00:06:25,560
Allow the melt temp apparatus to cool to 20 degrees below the melting point of the compound,

83
00:06:25,560 --> 00:06:32,760
and repeat the process two more times with the remaining samples to ensure reproducibility.

84
00:06:32,760 --> 00:06:37,240
Never re-melt a compound because it may have undergone a chemical change while heating such

85
00:06:37,240 --> 00:06:42,000
as oxidation, decomposition, or rearrangement.

86
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:46,840
The point where the first crystal starts to melt can seem ambiguous to someone taking

87
00:06:46,840 --> 00:06:49,440
a melting point for the first time.

88
00:06:49,440 --> 00:06:54,480
As stated earlier, this is the point where the first droplet of liquid forms.

89
00:06:54,480 --> 00:06:59,880
It is not when the material begins to settle, soften, or shrink.

90
00:06:59,880 --> 00:07:05,640
Only the appearance of a liquid droplet indicates the beginning of melting.

91
00:07:05,640 --> 00:07:10,600
One common use for melting point is to assess the purity of a known compound.

92
00:07:10,600 --> 00:07:15,280
If an impurity is present in a sample, it will interrupt the crystal lattice and weaken

93
00:07:15,280 --> 00:07:18,880
the forces of attraction that are holding the molecules together.

94
00:07:18,880 --> 00:07:24,000
Therefore, the sample will melt at a temperature that is lower than expected.

95
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:27,840
The sample will also melt over a wide temperature range.

96
00:07:27,840 --> 00:07:34,240
As a general guideline, if a compound melts over a range greater than 2 to 3 degrees Celsius,

97
00:07:34,240 --> 00:07:37,040
it is most likely impure.

98
00:07:37,040 --> 00:07:42,640
Another common application of melting point is the identification of an unknown crystalline

99
00:07:42,640 --> 00:07:43,640
solid.

100
00:07:43,640 --> 00:07:49,240
To identify a solid, simply compare the melting point of the unknown with the melting points

101
00:07:49,240 --> 00:07:52,480
of known compounds that are listed in a reference book.

102
00:07:52,480 --> 00:07:57,000
Typically, just the upper limit is reported in these reference books.

103
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:02,360
When combined with other spectroscopic data, you may be able to identify your compound.

104
00:08:02,360 --> 00:08:07,280
To confirm that your compound is indeed what you think, prepare three samples containing

105
00:08:07,280 --> 00:08:11,240
mixtures of your unknown compound and the proposed compound.

106
00:08:11,240 --> 00:08:18,240
20 to 80, 50-50, and 80 to 20 are good ratios to start with.

107
00:08:18,240 --> 00:08:20,840
Determine the melting point of each mixture.

108
00:08:20,840 --> 00:08:26,400
If all the mixtures melt at the same temperature and within the same range, you may have correctly

109
00:08:26,400 --> 00:08:28,600
identified your compound.

110
00:08:28,600 --> 00:08:33,840
If they do not, then you definitely have not identified your compound.

111
00:08:33,840 --> 00:08:38,120
There are several ways to obtain inaccurate melting point readings.

112
00:08:38,120 --> 00:08:42,400
The most common mistake is increasing the temperature too rapidly.

113
00:08:42,400 --> 00:08:46,600
In this situation, the capillary will not have enough time to equilibrate with the

114
00:08:46,600 --> 00:08:48,100
heated block.

115
00:08:48,100 --> 00:08:53,120
As a result, the melting range will appear narrower than it actually is, and the melting

116
00:08:53,120 --> 00:08:56,120
temperature will appear higher.

117
00:08:56,120 --> 00:09:02,120
A sample that disappears upon heating indicates that it is subliming instead of melting.

118
00:09:02,120 --> 00:09:07,160
To remedy this problem, seal the open end of the filled capillary with a Bunsen burner

119
00:09:07,160 --> 00:09:09,960
before placing it in the sample holder.

120
00:09:09,960 --> 00:09:16,000
A sample that changes color upon heating is probably undergoing decomposition.

121
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:20,360
Decomposition must be reported along with the melting point of the compound.

122
00:09:20,360 --> 00:09:25,160
If the melting point is still incorrect after troubleshooting, or if you need to confirm

123
00:09:25,160 --> 00:09:30,520
the accuracy of your result, you must calibrate the melt temp apparatus.

124
00:09:30,520 --> 00:09:35,800
To do this, find pure compounds with known but dissimilar melting points.

125
00:09:35,800 --> 00:09:43,760
This video will use naphthalene, n-phenylacetamide, salicylic acid, and succinic acid.

126
00:09:43,760 --> 00:09:48,640
Determine the melting point of each pure compound and compare the value you received

127
00:09:48,640 --> 00:09:50,440
with what is expected.

128
00:09:50,440 --> 00:09:56,120
Plot the actual melting point of each compound against its observed melting point.

129
00:09:56,120 --> 00:10:03,000
Use the resulting calibration curve to determine the expected melting point of your compound.

130
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:06,960
Determining the correct melting point of a crystal and solid involves good technique

131
00:10:06,960 --> 00:10:11,120
in both sample preparation and in taking the melting point.

132
00:10:11,120 --> 00:10:16,720
To ensure accurate results, use a minimal amount of solid, pack the crystals tightly

133
00:10:16,720 --> 00:10:21,440
together and increase the temperature slowly.

134
00:10:21,440 --> 00:10:25,640
Determining the melting point of a crystal and solid is the best way to assess its purity

135
00:10:25,640 --> 00:10:27,000
or establish its identity.
